-\abstract{Large language models (LLMs) increasingly pre-screen technical documents before an expert signs off, yet expert review knowledge enters such systems as keyword rules, prompt checklists or retrieval over past reviews, and each route loses a property that high-stakes review needs: findings must trace to a rule and a document location, decision authority must match how deterministically a requirement can be checked, knowledge must grow from use, and that growth must stay under human control. We call this the \emph{review-knowledge ingestion problem} and study it for preliminary review of technical solution proposals at a large utility. We present HARK (Human-Gated Authority-Typed Review Knowledge), which answers the four properties with four mechanisms rather than one. Knowledge becomes a versioned package of verifiability-typed rules: \emph{hard} rules are owned by deterministic verifiers that produce located evidence, including absence evidence an LLM may never assert; \emph{hybrid} rules let verifiers locate candidate spans while the LLM judges only inside them, enforced by a tool-side evidence gate; \emph{soft} rules only raise items for judgement. A deterministic policy derives the suggested verdict; only the expert writes the final one. Expert decisions become an audited learned layer that may tune parameters and phrasing but never promotes a rule's tier. Implemented as a plugin of an open-source agent harness (28 rules, no separate backend), the design is evaluated with a deterministic judge that decides only inside verifier-located spans; all numbers below are conditional on that judge, not on a particular large model. It reaches F1 0.798 against 0.222 for a keyword baseline on 100 synthetic proposals with 253 planted defects, with no finding raised on a clean proposal and 85\% verdict agreement ($\kappa=0.76$); 80\% of the remaining misses reach the expert as open items, at the cost of under-calling 15 of the 70 defective proposals, four of them to a pass. Only 10 of 28 rules proved deterministically verifiable, and the four blocking rules least of all.}
+\abstract{Rule-governed document review requires explicit criteria, source-addressable evidence, and a distinction between provisional findings and accountable human disposition. We present HARK, an architecture and an executed system that represent review criteria as rules with hard, hybrid, or soft operational authority. Deterministic verifiers produce checks and candidate spans; a positional evidence gate checks submitted quotations; a policy derives suggested verdicts; and decision records support feedback storage. Technical solution proposals are the sole empirical instantiation. On a fixed developmental regression corpus of 100 synthetic proposals with 253 planted defects, the combined configuration achieves micro-F1 0.798 versus 0.222 for a keyword baseline and 85\% policy-verdict agreement. Of 70 policy-non-Pass proposals, 15 are under-called and 10 receive Pass, including four of 23 expected Fail proposals. Sixty-eight of 85 misses enter pending review, without evidence of human resolution. Five development-team runs on three internal proposals demonstrate workflow and persistence, not independent review quality or learning gains. The evaluated snapshot does not enforce authenticated human approval, a complete effective-authority ceiling, or content-addressed configuration recovery. These remain target contracts rather than implemented guarantees. The contribution is an executable review architecture with bounded comparative results and explicit implementation boundaries.}
 
