-A rule package is a YAML file validated on load. Each rule has an identifier, a review dimension (capacity, security, interface, channel, data synchronisation, configuration, monitoring, documentation), a severity (blocker, major, minor), a \emph{tier}, the question put to the LLM and the expert, a rationale, a default remediation, an optional applicability precondition, a verifier specification and a status. The package also carries a lexicon---heading aliases, placeholder tokens, negation tokens and keyword families (e.g.\ \emph{loadtest} = load, stress and performance testing in Chinese)---which rules reference by key. Rules never embed vocabulary: the lexicon is the only place where language lives, so a package can be translated or extended without touching verifier code.
+\subsection{Operational Authority Types}\label{sec:rules}
 
-The tier is the type of the knowledge (Table~\ref{tab:tiers}). The criterion is operational: \emph{can a deterministic procedure produce located evidence for or against the requirement, including evidence of absence?} If yes for every aspect, the rule is hard; if a procedure can locate where the requirement would be addressed and flag surface cues (negation, placeholder, unjustified assertion) but the judgement requires reading, it is hybrid; if no procedure can locate relevant text reliably, it is soft. The three types differ not in confidence but in \emph{who is allowed to speak}, which is the point of the type: it is a permission, not a probability.
+Each YAML rule declares an identifier, dimension, severity, tier, question, rationale, remediation, applicability condition, verifier specification and status. A shared lexicon supplies heading aliases, placeholder and negation tokens, and keyword families. The package contains 28 rules: 10 hard, 13 hybrid and 5 soft. Seven have applicability preconditions; the lexicon contains 25 keyword families, 11 heading groups, 12 placeholder tokens and 11 negation tokens.
 
-A tier decides who may speak; it does not decide what is worth reading. Each rule therefore also carries an \emph{output channel}. Findings from opinion rules become numbered clauses of the formal opinion and are decided one by one. Findings from checklist rules---chapter completeness, annex numbering, table columns, sequence-diagram message counts---become a separate annex of correction items that the reviewer accepts or waives in a single action. Both channels are produced by the same verifiers and both count in the verdict policy (Section~\ref{sec:policy}); the split separates what blocks a proposal from what merely has to be tidied, so that a reviewer is not asked to adjudicate a dozen facts that are certainly true and rarely decisive.
+The tier is an \emph{operational routing declaration}. A hard rule uses a decisive implemented predicate; a hybrid rule combines deterministic checks or candidate localisation with a judgement step; a soft rule raises an unresolved item. Decisive execution of a predicate does not prove that the predicate fully captures the business requirement. For example, a lexical declaration of a load-test plan does not establish its scope, responsibilities, timing or performance criteria. Assigning a hard tier to that proxy cannot make those unmeasured properties automatically verifiable. Domain owners must first define the requirement and then specify which subpredicates the verifier actually covers.
 
