-Tool contracts are schema-checked with thirteen typed error codes, re-running a check after a decision is refused (it would orphan feedback), and an already reviewed proposal is resumed rather than duplicated. The engine's purity is what makes the offline evaluation of Section~\ref{sec:results} possible: the same functions that answer a live review are called by the evaluation script over a corpus, with the model replaced by a deterministic judge, so the mechanism is measured without model variance. On the reference machine the full corpus of 100 proposals $\times$ 23 verifiable rules is processed in about 1.6 s, well under a millisecond per rule--document pair, which is negligible next to the model latency of a single hybrid judgement; the review cost is dominated by the number of candidate spans that reach the model, not by the deterministic layer.
-
-The plugin is installed, like any other, as a profile plugin of the harness; the rule package is data, so a second domain is a second YAML file plus a lexicon, and rule edits take effect on the next review without a restart. The dashboard is served by the host's own web server under the host's access control, so no additional authentication surface is introduced; the left panel shows the review overview (calendar, trend, proposal list with in-place decision), the rule state (shipped, learned, candidates) and the evaluation runs, and a right sidebar tab keeps the active review beside the conversation.
+The retrospective analysis uses released outputs associated with repository snapshot \texttt{b566b08de2976451747215af4dc874a0846f6173}, and does not mix those results with the proposed approval/recovery contract. The stored outputs were reaggregated without changing inputs, labels, rules or runtime behaviour, so the reported figures describe that frozen snapshot rather than a later code state. A future rule, gate, policy or state-machine repair must be evaluated under a new frozen version; these historical detection scores do not certify it.
 
+The artifact supplies a test suite, and a clean-environment full-suite reproduction is not established here: the checked environment encountered missing host-peer dependencies and a packaging-ignore assertion failure. We therefore do not assert an all-passing published test count. Moving to another document domain requires new predicates, source adapters and labelled evaluation, not merely a second YAML file.
 
 \section{Results}\label{sec:results}
 
-\subsection{Setup}
+\subsection{Retrospective Evaluation Setup}
+
+\paragraph{Corpus and labels.}
+The released generator (seed 20261029) produced 100 synthetic proposals and 253 planted defects over 23 verifiable rules: 98 absent, 37 negated, 52 placeholder and 66 keyword-present-but-empty (hollow). Thirteen documents have no planted defect; 87 have at least one. Expected verdicts are derived from the planted defects using the frozen policy: 30 Pass, 47 Revise and 23 Fail. Seventeen of the 30 policy-Pass documents contain permissible minor defects, so ``policy Pass'' must not be used as a synonym for ``defect free''. Thirty generated proposals were shaped on the verdict distribution of 30 historical expert opinions; these are not the original private documents or independent expert labels for the generated text.
 
-\paragraph{Corpus.}
-The released generator (seed 20261029) produced 100 proposals with 253 planted defects over the 23 verifiable rules---98 \emph{absent}, 37 \emph{negated}, 52 \emph{placeholder} and 66 \emph{keyword-present-but-empty}---from phrase pools in which each rule has at least three satisfied and four unsatisfied phrasings, including at least one that avoids the lexicon keywords. Expected verdicts are 30 Pass, 47 Revise and 23 Fail, with 30 proposals shaped on the 15/14/1 verdict distribution of 30 historical expert opinions. The corpus is synthetic because the original proposals could not be released; it measures mechanism correctness and relative baselines, not business-semantic judgement. The released self-check recomputes, for every proposal, that each satisfied rule passes its verifier and that each planted rule fails it, so a reported false positive is attributable to the mechanism rather than to the corpus.
+The corpus and phrase pools were developed with the package. Its self-check guards selected execution-consistency conditions, including unexpected verifier failures on unplanted requirements, but does not require every planted defect to produce a verifier failure. Planted defects can remain pending or be missed. A generator-associated correction to the DOC-002 annex-number capture illustrates its value for development; it also means the corpus is developmental regression evidence, not an untouched semantic-review test set. The released labels are retained for this retrospective analysis, including the lifecycle and format ambiguities discussed in Section~\ref{sec:verifiers}.
 
-\paragraph{The corpus tested the shipped pack.}
-The self-check also caught a defect in the shipped package rather than in the baseline. The first generator run failed on every proposal that listed a prototype attachment: the annex-list pattern of DOC-002 captured the word after the annex numeral rather than the numeral itself, so the cross-reference resolver compared ``\textnormal{prototype}'' with ``\textnormal{1}'' and reported a correctly listed attachment as absent from the list. The pattern was corrected to capture the numeral, and the case is now part of the released self-check. A defect of this shape is invisible in a single review and unmissable across a hundred, which is the practical argument for shipping the corpus next to the rules.
+\paragraph{Configurations.}
+B0 uses rule-specific substrings without the structural and negation handling of the combined system, applying the same aggregation policy to its outputs. S-full uses the verifiers, candidate routes, gate and policy, with a deterministic \emph{flag judge}: it proposes a finding when a candidate carries a negation, assertion-only or placeholder cue. It does not interpret design adequacy. S$-$verifier widens the hybrid statement search to the whole document. B0 does not invoke this judge; the comparison changes multiple mechanisms and cannot isolate a causal typing effect. Memory is present in the software but these outputs do not measure a treatment effect of recalled feedback. No live LLM configuration was run for the reported corpus scores.
 
-\paragraph{Configurations and metrics.}
-We compare B0, which replicates the 2025 prototype (3--5 substrings per rule, no negation detection, no structure, no evidence) but applies the same verdict policy more leniently than the prototype, with the typed design S-full (verifiers, candidates, gate, policy, memory) and with S$-$verifier, where hybrid statements are evaluated over the whole document. The plugin ships no model client; hybrid judgements go through a pluggable judge. We report the deterministic \emph{flag judge}, which raises a finding on a candidate span exactly when the verifier marked it negated, assertion-only or placeholder. It measures the mechanism, not LLM capability; the LLM-judge configurations (direct prompting, gate disabled, model-stated verdict) are left to a companion report rather than approximated. Alignment is at (review, rule) granularity, and we separate two quantities that are often conflated: \emph{in-pack agreement}, a predicted rule that matches a planted defect or a satisfied requirement, and \emph{out-of-pack additions}, expert findings for which no rule exists. Only the first is comparable with the baseline; the second is reported separately as candidate rules (Section~\ref{sec:memory}) and never folded into precision or $\kappa$. We report precision, recall, F1 with bootstrap intervals over proposals, severity-weighted recall (weights 3/2/1), verdict accuracy, macro-F1, Cohen's $\kappa$ unweighted and quadratically weighted~\cite{cohen1960,landis1977}, evidence validity (share of model registrations that passed the gate) and pending coverage (share of misses shown to the expert as open items). Three repetitions of each configuration produced byte-identical metrics, so the intervals are bootstrap intervals over proposals, not repetition variance.
+\paragraph{Metrics and analysis provenance.}
+Alignment is by (document, rule). We recomputed micro-precision, micro-recall and micro-F1 from summed true positives (TP), false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN), and mean per-document F1 from the stored outputs. An empty truth and empty prediction receives per-document F1 of one. We also recomputed policy-verdict accuracy and unweighted Cohen's $\kappa$~\cite{cohen1960,landis1977}, gate acceptance, and referral of missed planted defects to pending review. Out-of-package additions and soft-rule items do not enter the 23-rule detection scores. Published deterministic repetitions have identical core outputs and do not provide independent samples. The analysis reaggregates the stored records; it neither regenerates the corpus nor reruns the engine, and reported statistics are descriptive rather than inferential. The document is the observational unit; neither 253 defects nor 2300 rule--document pairs are independent document samples.
 
