-\subsection{Typing versus Keywording (RQ2)}
-
-Both configurations are judged by the same deterministic, structure-only judge, so the comparison isolates the mechanism rather than model behaviour; what a large model would add or lose inside the same spans is left to the ablation named in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}. B0 produced 47 true, 124 false and 206 missed findings; S-full produced 168 true findings, no false finding and 85 misses, a gain of $\Delta$F1 $=+0.564$ (paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over per-proposal F1, $p<0.001$; McNemar on verdict correctness $b/c=64/7$, $p<0.001$; both survive Holm correction). S-full reaches F1 $=1.0$ on 42 of 100 proposals and its per-proposal F1 has median 0.857.
-
-The aggregate hides an asymmetry that matters more than F1. No clean proposal receives a finding, but the policy is not symmetrical in its errors: 15 defective proposals are under-called and none is over-called, and of the 23 proposals that should have failed, four are suggested as a pass and five as a revision. The verdict error is therefore one-sided---the design is conservative about what it asserts and permissive about what it lets through---and the mistakes concentrate where the rules are hybrid: 18 of the 35 blocking defects that the corpus plants are never confirmed, which is what a 0.486 blocker recall means in counts. A reader who only sees ``no false positive'' would miss that a review can look clean while a blocking defect remains unadjudicated.
+\subsection{Combined-Configuration Trade-offs (RQ2)}
 
-B0's errors have the shape the typing predicts, and the shape is quantitative. On the 13 rules whose keyword is uninformative (negated, placeholder or hollow phrasings dominate), B0's precision is exactly 0.00; all 124 of its false positives come from six rules, three of which key on vocabulary that satisfied text uses just as often (encryption, channel identifiers, rate limiting). Recall is no better: below 0.15 on 15 of 23 rules, including the sequence-diagram rule (0.13) and the load-test rule (0.29), because the satisfied phrasings often avoid the lexicon---the capacity drill that never says ``load test''. Its verdict agreement is at chance ($\kappa=-0.04$; 17 of 30 Pass proposals rated Fail), reproducing the 2025 failure in which a declared-but-unverifiable checklist drove the verdict.
-
-Table~\ref{tab:tiers-results} breaks the same findings down by tier and severity. The tier result is the mechanism claim of the paper: hard rules are decided with precision 1.000 and recall 0.880, hybrid rules with precision 1.000 and recall 0.387; under B0 the corresponding precisions are 0.698 and 0.085. Typing thus does not merely improve the aggregate; it removes the failure mode that produced false findings, and what remains is a recall cost that is concentrated where judgement is genuinely needed.
+Micro-F1 rises from 0.2217 to 0.7981, a difference of 0.5764. Mean per-document F1 rises from 0.2161 to 0.7798, a paired mean difference of 0.5636. The earlier rounded 0.564 is therefore a different aggregation, not the micro-F1 difference. S-full has perfect per-document F1 on 42 documents and median F1 0.857. The improvement supports this combined configuration over this simple keyword baseline on shared developmental templates. It does not establish superiority over richer deterministic baselines, arbitrary prompted models or human review.
 
