+\subsection{A Historical Walkthrough and Its Label Boundaries}
 
+The generated proposal \texttt{syn-hist-30} concerns a balance-query capability opened to third-party channels. Its three gate-confirmed blockers are placeholder concurrency control (CAP-001), a negated binding-relation check (SEC-002), and placeholder exposure-risk assessment (SEC-004). A fourth confirmed finding is the hard diagram rule DOC-001, while MON-001 remains pending. The stored expected and suggested verdicts are both Fail, and per-document F1 is 0.889. The automated hybrid actor in this corpus case is the flag judge, not an LLM.
 
-\subsection{The Protocol Closed on Internal Proposals}\label{sec:pilot}
+Two parts of this case cannot be interpreted as independent evidence of business defects. DOC-001 fails an image-only diagram under the frozen Mermaid predicate, without assessing what the image shows. MON-001 treats ``New page tracking points must be added to count usage'' as lacking an implementation statement. For a proposal before construction, that may instead be an implementation plan whose completeness needs review; lack of completed deployment is not automatically a proposal defect. We retain these historical labels to keep the analysis version-consistent, but identify the task/label discrepancies. Domain-owner adjudication and a new frozen evaluation are required before treating a corrected predicate as validated.
 
-Beyond the corpus we ran the system on three technical solution proposals written for the platform, one reviewed twice after revision and once re-run to exercise resumption: five runs in total. The runs were performed by the team that built the system, so they document mechanics, not an independent expert study. Across the five runs the system raised 58 findings: 40 located by verifiers, 12 judged by the model inside candidate spans, and 6 added by the operator. The gate refused 3 of the 12 model-judged findings, all with the same reason---the quote fell outside the spans the verifier had located---so the refusal path of Section~\ref{sec:gate} was exercised on real prose and not only on planted defects. Hard and typed rules rated every proposal \emph{fail}; the two reviews carried to a final verdict kept it, the operator accepting 15 of the 18 findings that required a decision and rejecting 3, none rewritten. The learned layer was written: after five runs it held statistics for 13 rules and 21 exemplars, and no rule had changed tier.
+\subsection{Development-Team Workflow Evidence (RQ4)}\label{sec:pilot}
+
+The authors report five runs on three private technical proposals: one revised/reviewed again and one resumption run. Only two reviews reached a final verdict. Across these runs they report 58 findings: 40 verifier-located, 12 model-judged and six operator additions; three of the 12 model-judged submissions were rejected for out-of-candidate quotations. They also report 18 decisions (15 accept, three dismiss, no rewrite), statistics for 13 rules and 21 exemplars. These categories describe the authors' workflow account, not a reconstructed private-store audit or an independent expert study. The private source documents, model/prompt configuration and full approval/decision trace are not available for independent verification here.
+
+The account supports feasibility of recording and aggregating feedback in ordinary operator-led use. It does not establish verified consent for every record, guaranteed pending disposition, reduced workload, independent reviewer acceptance or a learning curve. It also does not repair the callable-interface and configuration-recovery counterexamples described in Section~\ref{sec:contract}. An anonymous trace tying inputs, gate outcomes, dispositions and effective configuration to each final opinion would be needed to strengthen this workflow evidence without publishing confidential content.
 
 \section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
 
 \subsection{Answers to the Research Questions}
 
-\paragraph{RQ1: the verifiability tension.}
-In this corpus the tension is not hypothetical but measurable, and it has a direction: the rules that decide a release are the rules least decidable by structure. All four blocker rules are hybrid; recall on blockers (0.486) is below recall on majors (0.702) and minors (0.675). A checklist that is tuned to be fully automatic will therefore be automated and lenient exactly where leniency is expensive, while a checklist tuned to be strict about blockers will produce machine findings that a human must adjudicate anyway. The three-way typing is the smallest mechanism we found that makes this tension explicit instead of average it away: it assigns each rule an authority path first, and only then measures the path.
+\paragraph{RQ1: representation, operational authority, and implementation boundaries.}
+HARK makes review criteria inspectable as rule records with evidence expectations and operational routes. The package contains 10 hard, 13 hybrid, and five soft rules; all four blocker rules are hybrid. These tiers identify execution paths in this package, not intrinsic decidability of the corresponding business requirements. A successful lexical check establishes its observable predicate, not the adequacy of a load-testing plan or security design. The design separates verifier-produced findings, gate-confirmed findings, expert adoption, and final disposition. The evaluated snapshot implements parts of this separation but does not enforce the complete target authority contract: gate-confirmed blockers already affect the suggested verdict, and some hybrid structural failures are produced directly by verifiers.
 
-\paragraph{RQ2: what typing buys.}
-Typing removed the failure mode rather than merely reducing it. Under B0 the association between rule declarations and reported findings is broken in both directions: 124 false findings and 206 misses, verdicts at chance. Under S-full, no finding lacks a located quote, and recall rises to 0.664 with severity weighting at 0.649 and verdict agreement at $\kappa=0.76$. The interesting part is not the F1 gap but its composition: the gain is concentrated on negated (2.7\% $\rightarrow$ 83.8\%) and placeholder phrasings (0\% $\rightarrow$ 80.8\%), that is, on text that contains the keyword and means the opposite of it. Keyword logic fails there by construction; structure alone recovers most of it, and the model is needed for the rest.
+\paragraph{RQ2: fixed-corpus detection and verdict trade-offs.}
+The combined S-full configuration produces 168 true positives, no false positives, and 85 false negatives, compared with 47, 124, and 206 for B0. Micro-F1 increases from 0.222 to 0.798, a difference of 0.576; the mean paired per-proposal F1 difference is 0.564, a distinct aggregation. This is a bounded improvement on a developmental synthetic regression corpus. B0 and S-full differ in detectors, cue handling, candidate routing, and evidence checks, and B0 does not use the flag judge. The comparison therefore does not isolate typing's causal effect. Identical S-full and S$-$verifier results show that this deterministic judge does not expose an effect of wider span scoping, not that scoping is effective or unnecessary for an LLM.
 
-\paragraph{RQ3: whether evidence changes behaviour.}
-The gate did not make the mechanism more accurate; it made it auditable, and the audit is what changed our own behaviour. One registration in five failed the gate, and the failure was always a property of the evidence rather than of the finding: too short a quote, never a mislocated one, because the deterministic judge quotes the span it was given. We therefore do not claim that the gate improves precision in this configuration---precision was already saturated---but that it produces a signal a reviewer can act on, and that it makes the weakest link in the chain (a model that quotes from memory) visible as a rejection rather than as an unverifiable claim. Whether it improves a real model's precision is the first question of the planned LLM-judge ablation.
+The verdict trade-off is one-sided but not benign. Agreement with policy-derived labels is 85\%. Of 70 policy-non-Pass documents, 15 are under-called and 10 receive Pass: six expected Revise and four expected Fail. The latter four belong to a subset of 23 expected Fail documents. The corpus contains 13 zero-defect documents and 87 with at least one defect; the 30 policy-Pass documents are a different group, including 17 with minor defects. No finding is raised on the 13 zero-defect documents. These results describe the specified policy and labels, not reliable release decisions in operational use.
 
-\paragraph{RQ4: whether expert decisions can be the knowledge.}
-The mechanism stores decisions, not preferences: each review decision is written with an issue identifier, a verdict, an optional rewrite and a timestamp, and the aggregate recall per rule is a query over those rows, not a retrained parameter. That design has a cost we did not pay down---no expert reviewed the 100 synthetic proposals, so the memory layer is exercised only by the deterministic judge---and a benefit we can state precisely: the memory cannot drift from the rules, because it is keyed by the same rule identifiers, and an expert can see and correct any single row. Where the learning lives is therefore a design choice with an audit trail, not an emergent property of a model.
+\paragraph{RQ3: traceability, gate acceptance, and unresolved workload.}
+The flag judge submits 53 hybrid findings, of which 42 pass the gate and 11 fail the quotation-length floor. Gate acceptance is 79.2\%, not semantic evidence validity. The 43 hybrid true positives comprise those 42 accepted submissions plus one directly detected structural failure. The current location predicate checks overlap with a candidate span; neither overlap nor a genuine quotation establishes that the text supports the allegation. Zero false positives in this configuration are an empirical result, not a consequence of positional grounding.
 
-\subsection{Why Asymmetric Authority}
+Of 85 missed defects, 68 appear in pending review and 17 remain unsurfaced. The complete pending workload is 932 unique hybrid document--rule pairs, a mean of 9.32 per document and median of nine, excluding soft-rule items. Only 68 of these pairs match planted defects. This label density is not pending-item precision: pending expresses uncertainty, not an asserted defect. Referral coverage of 80\% of misses provides no evidence that a reviewer read, resolved, or corrected them. A suggested Pass based on eligible findings is consequently not equivalent to a completed review.
 
-The arithmetic of the two directions is not symmetric. A false finding costs reviewer trust and, if it reaches the formal letter, an argument with the submitting team; enough of them end the deployment. A missed finding costs one line in the reviewer's own memory---provided the reviewer still has the habit of reading the document, which the pending list is designed to keep. The policy encodes that asymmetry: the system may conclude \emph{Fail} only from confirmed blockers, but it can always be overruled, and it never writes the final verdict. This is also why we do not use the model's own verdict anywhere in the policy: a model-stated verdict is a single point of failure with no audit trail, whereas the policy's inputs are rows a human can inspect one by one.
+\paragraph{RQ4: persistence, approval, and recovery.}
+The reported five development-team runs on three internal proposals, with two reaching a final verdict, provide workflow evidence. The reported 58 findings and subsequent statistics and exemplars describe recorded activity, not independently audited quality or learning gains. Decision records can be persisted and aggregated without establishing that their origin is authenticated or that they improve later reviews. The evaluated final-verdict path accepts a reviewer string, and instruction quotations for selected edits are length-checked rather than verified as human authorisation. Pending findings can remain when a review is finalised.
 
-The asymmetry has a limit we should state. It assumes the reviewer reads the pending list. In our walkthrough the missed tracking defect is one item in a list of five, and a reviewer who trusts the four confirmed findings will sign a Fail for the right reason and still never see the fifth. The interface, not the policy, carries that risk; the plugin addresses it by making pending items first-class objects in the review store and by requiring the reviewer's confirmation to be recorded per item, but a field study is needed to know whether that is enough.
+The authority and recovery gaps extend beyond tier editing. Changes to no-candidate behaviour, draft activation, finding severity, and reset can alter effective decision behaviour without changing a stored tier. The displayed \texttt{pack@ver+ovN} summary counts overridden rules rather than identifying their contents; different behaviours can share the same label. Authenticated approval bound to the action and effective content, control of all authority-changing operations, and immutable configuration snapshots are target contracts, not capabilities demonstrated by this snapshot. The observed counterexamples concern callable interfaces and do not establish malicious activity in the internal runs.
 
-\subsection{Position Relative to Automated Reviewers}
+\subsection{Implications for Evidence-Bearing Document Review}
 
-Recent work has largely measured automated review as a text-generation task: given a diff or a design, produce comments that a developer accepts~\cite{pornprasit2024,rovo2026}. That framing optimises for agreeableness, and the largest industrial deployment we know of reports acceptance by developers on individual review comments, a metric that suits a diff and a comment stream rather than a verdict. Our framing is the complement: the artifact is not a comment stream but a verdict with an auditable derivation, and the unit of correctness is a rule, which makes the system comparable with a compliance checklist rather than with a human reviewer. The two framings meet where the checklist itself is written in prose: neuro-symbolic verification of design documents~\cite{neuro2026} shares our impulse to constrain the model with a structure, but uses the structure to check a model's extraction; we use it to decide who is allowed to speak.
+The useful abstraction is a review record linking a criterion, an evidence object, an operational route, and a disposition. This complements comment-generation approaches and evidence-aware review workflows~\cite{alsawalqah2026,pornprasit2024}: it makes the inputs to a recommendation inspectable without treating inspectability as correctness. Human approval remains a design requirement whose engineering implementation must be distinguished from ordinary operator-led workflow.
 
-The compliance context also sets a constraint we have not fully exploited. Security-requirement reviews and certification audits are already organised around control catalogues~\cite{secreq2025,cyberreq2026}, and a traced requirement is the artifact those processes treat as evidence~\cite{trace2026}. A rule pack whose identifiers match the catalogue would let a review quote a control number directly, and would let the memory aggregate at the level a certification body cares about. We keep the rule schema aligned with that possibility (each rule carries a dimension and a provenance field) but leave the mapping to future work, because it changes the ontology of a review, not its implementation.
-
-Two risks of this framing deserve naming. The first is regulatory: if a review supports a decision that a regulation covers, the artefact and its provenance may acquire legal weight, which is why the AI Act's documentation duties~\cite{aiact2024} are a plausible next constraint on the design. The second is the model itself: a judge that reads untrusted proposals is a prompt-injection target~\cite{shi2024inject}, and a memory that accumulates expert rewrites is a privacy target~\cite{mempriv2025}. Neither is solved by the gate; both are reasons to keep the model's authority narrow, which is the design's main defence.
+The same abstraction could support assurance cases, compliance dossiers, or architecture designs if each supplies explicit criteria and addressable evidence. Such adaptation would require domain-specific evidence objects, lifecycle assumptions, rule provenance, and disposition responsibilities. This study validates none of those additional applications. Nor does narrow model input eliminate prompt injection or feedback-storage privacy risks~\cite{shi2024inject,mempriv2025}. The present evidence supports the architecture's executable composition and its fixed-corpus trade-offs, while exposing the limits that govern its interpretation.
 
 \section{Limitations and Future Work}\label{sec:limitations}
 
-\paragraph{The corpus is synthetic.}
-The 100 proposals are generated from phrase pools, so they measure mechanism correctness against planted defects, not judgement quality against real design intent. Three consequences are unavoidable: a satisfied phrasing is satisfied by construction, the vocabulary is narrower than field text, and the verdict labels are correct by construction rather than by expert agreement. We mitigate the third by planting defects at the clause level and deriving the expected verdict from the policy itself (a proposal is Fail iff a blocker is planted), so accuracy measures the policy's execution rather than a taste. The mitigation does not remove the gap; a corpus of anonymised real proposals with two independent expert labels is the natural next step, and the anonymisation procedure is already specified in the released pipeline.
+\paragraph{Developmental synthetic evaluation.}
+The corpus reuses phrase pools and was developed alongside the rule package. Its planted labels and expected verdicts reflect the generator and the same policy used for evaluation; the self-check is an execution-consistency check that allows planted defects to remain pending or missed. The comparison is therefore a regression result, not an independent estimate of semantic review accuracy. Documents are the resampling unit; 253 defects and 2300 verifiable rule--document pairs are not independent document samples, and byte-identical deterministic repetitions add no sampling evidence. The reported statistics are descriptive, and no confidence intervals or significance tests are claimed. Generalisation would require frozen development artefacts and independently labelled, source-separated material.
 
-\paragraph{The judge is deterministic.}
-Hybrid recall (0.387) is a lower bound, since the flag judge decides only on structural cues and leaves semantic cases pending by design; conversely precision (1.000) is an upper bound, because the same judge never invents a quote. Span scoping showed no effect precisely because the judge only cites the span it is given, so the effect that spans are meant to have---keeping a generative model from wandering---is untested here. We did not measure stability across models or prompts: no number in this paper says how two large models, or one model at two temperatures, would disagree on the same candidate span. The planned ablation covers three judge settings (direct prompting over the whole document, prompting over candidate spans, and prompting with the gate disabled) with two open models, fixed decoding parameters and the same corpus, and reports the gate's rejection taxonomy as the primary outcome.
+\paragraph{Judge and component attribution.}
+The flag judge reacts to verifier cues rather than interpreting design intent. Its recall and precision are neither general lower nor upper bounds for an LLM, which could both recover semantic defects and introduce unsupported findings. The end-to-end comparison changes multiple components and does not identify typing's individual effect. No reported experiment establishes cross-model stability, semantic entailment at the gate, or the benefit of span scoping for generative judges. Controlled component tests and model-based evaluations are possible extensions, not results of this study.
 
-\paragraph{No independent expert was in the loop.}
-The online form of the contribution---that review decisions become memory, and that memory improves later reviews---is not tested. The five internal runs of Section~\ref{sec:pilot} show that the loop closes and that the learned layer is written, but the operator was the team that wrote the rules, so their acceptance rate says nothing about an outside expert, and five runs cannot measure a learning curve. What the runs exercise is the plumbing (decision recording, memory aggregation, exemplar retrieval) and the offline aggregator, not the claim that expert recall per rule rises with exposure. That claim needs a longitudinal deployment: the same reviewers, the same capability, a baseline period without memory and a treatment period with it, with per-rule recall, dismissal rate and rewrite rate as the outcomes. We describe the measurement plan and the schema rather than report the effect, and we consider this the paper's largest open question.
+\paragraph{Human approval and effective authority.}
+The evaluated implementation assumes a trusted operator-led workflow; it does not authenticate human approval through its reviewer-name or instruction-quotation fields. Pending issues can survive finalisation. Existing tier restrictions do not cover the full effective-authority surface, including parameter changes, draft activation, finding-level severity, and reset. A stricter implementation would need approvals bound to a trusted principal, action, object, effective content, and consumption state, together with explicit disposition or waiver of unresolved items. These controls are planned and have not been implemented or validated here. Their absence limits assurance claims but does not imply that the internal runs involved misuse.
 
-\paragraph{Scope of the rule pack.}
-Twenty-eight rules covering one capability's review checklist are not a review standard. The typing mechanism is agnostic to the rules---it takes any rule with a declared verifier---but the composition table (Section~\ref{sec:rules}) shows that the balance of tiers is a property of the domain: documentation rules are mostly mechanical, security rules are mostly semantic. A different domain will need a different balance, and the question of how to choose tiers without taste is open; our practice was to write the verifier first and the tier second, which makes the choice traceable but not automatic.
+\paragraph{Version recovery and feedback use.}
+The override-count version label is a display summary, not a unique content identifier. It cannot alone reproduce a past review or distinguish different configurations with the same number of overridden rules. The target recovery contract would preserve the effective rules, lexicon, overrides, policy, parser and verifier versions, and an audit cursor in an immutable snapshot. Current persistence and aggregation evidence does not establish complete historical replay, authenticated attribution of every update, or net benefits from feedback. No longitudinal improvement claim is made.
 
-\paragraph{Language and lexicon.}
-The corpus, the lexicon and the rules are in Chinese, for the electricity-service deployment; the mechanism is language-agnostic but the cue lists are not. Porting is a configuration task, not a code change, and the lexicon is versioned with the pack, but each port needs its own self-check, because a cue list calibrated on one domain's phrasing fails silently on another's, and only a per-domain self-check catches that.
+\paragraph{Domain, lifecycle, and input coverage.}
+The 28-rule Chinese-language package concerns one organisation's technical proposal review. Hard denotes the snapshot's automatic route and may use a lexical or structural proxy narrower than the business criterion. Requiring Mermaid in the current package reflects a parser-visible check; an unreadable image does not by itself prove a defective design without a governing format requirement. Likewise, future-tense implementation plans must be judged against proposal-stage criteria rather than treated as failed implementation acceptance. Changing these predicates, labels, tiers, or evidence rules would require revalidation and, where affected, recomputation; the current metrics cannot be reassigned to a revised task. Other languages and document families require their own evidence mapping and validation.
 
-\paragraph{Verdict policy and human factors.}
-The verdict policy is deterministic and its threshold is insensitive in this corpus, but it assumes a reviewer who reads pending items, and we have not measured that. Nor have we measured whether an expert overrides the suggested verdict more often when it is wrong in the lenient direction, which is the asymmetry the design leans on. Both are study designs, not implementation gaps, and both belong with the longitudinal deployment.
+\paragraph{Workflow evidence and human factors.}
+Three internal documents and five development-team runs, only two carried to a final verdict, are insufficient to establish external reviewer acceptance, reduced workload, or better decisions. The reported counts are not an independent audit of the private pilot store. Pending referral is not human resolution, and agreement between suggested and final verdicts is not a direct measure of rubber-stamping. Human disposition quality, time costs, missed pending items, and automation bias remain unmeasured. Evaluation of these outcomes must also distinguish provisional recommendation from review completion.
 
 \section{Conclusions}
 
-A review system built on large language models tends to give the model one of two roles: generate the comments, or decide the verdict. We have argued for a third arrangement in which the model never decides alone, the program never decides what it cannot check, and the expert's decisions are the knowledge the system accumulates. Its unit of construction is the rule, its unit of evidence is the quote, its unit of authority is the tier, and its unit of learning is the decision row.
+HARK represents rule-governed, evidence-bearing document review through explicit criteria, operational authority routes, located evidence, provisional findings, and decision records. Technical solution proposals are the sole empirical instantiation; assurance cases, compliance dossiers, and architecture designs remain potential applications. The system contribution is the executable combination of these review objects and interfaces, not a guarantee that a model, verifier, or recorded operator name establishes an accountable final decision.
 
-On a corpus of 100 proposals with 253 planted defects, the arrangement produces no false finding, recovers two thirds of the defects, under-calls 15 of the 70 defective proposals (four to a pass) and agrees with the planted verdict on 85\% of proposals ($\kappa=0.76$), against a keyword baseline at chance. The more instructive result is where it loses: the rules that block a release are the rules whose satisfaction needs a reader, and the misses that remain are concentrated on prose that plans rather than implements. The loop also closed outside the corpus: five runs on three internal proposals, 58 findings and 18 operator decisions, with the gate refusing three model-judged findings on real prose. We read all of this as evidence that the design constraint is right---the model's job is to read, not to conclude---and as the agenda that follows: a judge worth trusting, a longitudinal test of whether decisions improve later reviews, and a mapping between rule packs and the control catalogues that compliance work already uses.
+On the fixed synthetic regression corpus, the combined configuration achieves micro-F1 0.798 versus 0.222 for B0, with 168 true positives, no false positives, and 85 misses. Policy-verdict agreement is 85\%; 15 of 70 policy-non-Pass documents are under-called, including 10 suggested Pass and four Fail-to-Pass cases among 23 expected Fail documents. Sixty-eight misses enter pending review, without demonstrated human resolution. The reported development-team runs support workflow and feedback persistence but not independent quality or online learning benefits. Authenticated approval, a complete effective-authority ceiling, and content-addressed recovery remain unimplemented target contracts. These boundaries define what the present design and validation evidence supports.
 
